 player's game history includes Play F4, etc. The player across has Play F2 in their history. Maybe not a big threat. J3: I have one J3. The unshown J3 has 1 left. If I play J3, someone could take it for a Peng. But the player across has J3 in their history (Play J3, Play F2, etc.), so maybe they have a J3 already.\n\nNow, considering my hand. I need to maximize my points and avoid giving others a chance to win. Let's think about my current hand. I have a Chow of B3-B4-B5, a Pung of W3-W3-W3. The remaining tiles are W6, W7, T3, T7, B3 (two), F2, J3. I need to form four melds and a pair. Currently, I have two melds. So I need two more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\nIf I can form a Pung or Chow with the remaining tiles. I have two B3s. If I can get another B3, that would make a Pung. But I can't draw, I have to play. Alternatively, maybe I can form a Chow with T3 and T7. T3 is part of a possible Chow with T4 and T5, but I don't have those. T7 could be part of T6-T7-T8, but I don't have T6 or T8. So maybe not. W6 and W7 could form a sequence if there's a W5 or W8. But I don't have W5 in my hand (I have W3 pung and W6 in hand). Wait, the unshown tiles show W5 as 0, meaning all W5s are already out. So W5 is already discarded. So if I have W6 and W7, maybe someone could take W7 for a Chi if they have W5 and W8. But since W5 is already out, maybe not. Alternatively, if I have W6, maybe someone can take it for a Pung if they have two W6s. The next player has a Pung of W4-W5-W6, so they have one W6. If they have another W6, they could Peng. But the unshown W6 is 1, meaning one left. So if I play W6, there's a chance they can Peng.\n\nLooking at my hand, the best option might be to play a tile that is less likely to be needed by others and that helps me form more melds. Let's see:\n\nIf I play B3, that leaves me with one B3. If I can form a Pung of B3s, but I need another B3. But since the unshown B3 has 2 left, maybe that's possible. But I don't have control over drawing. A